Based on the visual analysis of the chest X-ray, there are no visible metal implants (such as pacemakers, sternal wires, or prosthetic valves) present in the thoracic cavity. The image shows patchy bilateral infiltrates but lacks any high-density radiopaque objects characteristic of metal hardware.

Therefore, a patient with such an anatomy (or generally, patients with most types of non-ferromagnetic or stable ferromagnetic implants) can typically undergo a CXR. While MRI is strictly contraindicated for many metal implants due to heating and movement risks, Chest X-rays use ionizing radiation and do not pose the same magnetic risks; thus, they are safe and routinely performed on patients with implants. Since this specific patient does not even appear to have an implant, the answer is definitively yes.

